course: dictionary
lesson: 6255
title: Words from food and history
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to a special kind of thing, like food or a story. Cooks and writers use them, and they can help you work out a meaning. «They live at the bottom of a well, on treacle.» Treacle is something you can eat, because people live on food, so it must be a food.
explain: Molasses is a word you may meet in a recipe for gingerbread or toffee. The recipe tells you it is a thick, dark, sticky syrup. Read the words around it: thick, dark and sticky are clues that help you picture it.
explain: Some words change with place. In the UK, molasses is called black treacle. In the USA, people say «slow as molasses (in January)», which means very slow. Alice in Wonderland also has treacle, in a well. Always check what the words around it show before you decide.
fact: Molasses is a thick, dark, sticky syrup.
fact: Molasses is made when sugar is taken from sugar cane or sugar beet.
fact: In the UK, molasses is called black treacle.
fact: Molasses is used in cooking, for example in gingerbread, toffee and baked beans.
fact: In history, molasses was part of the trade across the Atlantic, including the cruel slave trade.
exercise: «They live at the bottom of a well, on treacle.» What is treacle? | * Thick, sweet syrup, like golden syrup or molasses | Cold water from the river | A fish that lives in wells | A dry, crunchy biscuit
exercise: In the USA, people say «slow as molasses (in January)». What does it mean? | * Very slow | Very cold | Very fast | Very sweet
exercise: In the UK, molasses is also called what? | Golden fish | Cane water | Sugar beet | * Black treacle
test: Molasses is made when sugar is taken from which two plants? | Tea leaves or coffee beans | Apples or pears | * Sugar cane or sugar beet | Wheat or barley
test: Which words describe molasses? | Cold, clear and fizzy | * Thick, dark and sticky | Light, dry and crunchy | Pale and watery
exam: Ella reads «a spoon of thick, dark, sticky molasses» in a recipe. What does molasses mean? | A bright red flower | * A thick, dark, sticky syrup | A kind of sand | A sweet, dry biscuit
